DUNZIP(1)                      ZOAU Command Syntax                     DUNZIP(1)

NAME

    dunzip - Unterse and restore datasets from a dzip archive file or dataset.

SYNOPSIS

  	dunzip [OPTIONS] SOURCE [VOLUME]

    Restores datasets from a dzip archive dataset or file. SOURCE can be either
    a dataset name or a z/OS UNIX file path.

    You can specify some options with either a short or long flag. If an option
    also requires an argument, you must specify the argument along with your
    chosen flag type.
    For example: -e <DATASET_PATTERN> or --exclude=<DATASET_PATTERN>

    --admin
        Sets the ADMINISTRATOR keyword for the ADRDSSU RESTORE command. Acts as
        a DFSMSdss storage administrator. For administrators, DFSMSdss
        bypasses access checking for datasets and catalogs.

    --bypass-acs=<DATASET_NAME>
        Specifies that Automatic Class Selection (ACS) routines are
        not to be invoked to determine the target dataset's storage class or
        management class names. Equivalent to BYPASSACS command keyword.

    -d, --debug
        Print out debug messages.

    -D, --dataset
        SOURCE is a dataset name. If omitted, SOURCE is a z/OS UNIX filename.

    -e, --exclude=<DATASET_PATTERN>
        Specifies the name of a dataset to be excluded from the datasets
        selected by the INCLUDE keyword. Either a fully or a partially
        qualified dataset name can be used. Equivalent to EXCLUDE command
        keyword.

    -h, --help
        Display syntax help.

    -H, --newhlq=<NEWHLQ>
        Specifies the high-level-qualifier (HLQ) to use for the restored
        datasets. If not specified, then the username of the currently
        logged-in user is used as the HLQ. This argument is ignored if the
        -N or --no-rename arguments are provided.

    -i, --include=<DATASET_PATTERN>
        Specifies the name of a dataset eligible to be copied. Either a fully
        or a partially qualified dataset name can be used. Equivalent to
        INCLUDE command keyword.

    -I, --import
        Specifies that the datasets that are being restored were dumped from a
        different system and should thus be considered new datasets.
        Equivalent to IMPORT command keyword.

    --keep-temporary-dataset
        Do not delete the temporary dataset produced by untersing the input
        archive file or dataset. This can reduce processing time if the
        dataset is used for multiple dunzip invocations.

        Note that the dataset will need to be manually deleted when no longer
    needed.

    --keyword=<KEYWORD>
        Specifies an ADRDSSU RESTORE command keyword. Use quotes to pass special
        characters, such as parentheses. Use the --keyword argument repeatedly
        to specify multiple keywords. See the DESCRIPTION section for a link
        to the ADRDSSU documentation with keyword explanations.

    -m, --management-class=<MANAGEMENT_CLASS>
        Specifies the user-desired management class that is to replace the
        source management class as input to the ACS routines. Equivalent to
        MGMTCLAS command keyword.

    --null-management-class
        Specifies that the input to the ACS routines is a null management class
        rather than the source dataset's management class. Equivalent to
        NULLMGMTCLAS command keyword.

    --null-storage-class
        Specifies that the input to the ACS routines is to be a null storage
        class rather than the source dataset's storage class. Equivalent to
        NULLSTORCLAS command keyword.

    -N, --no-rename
        Restore datasets to their original HLQ from when they were archived.
        This overrides any value specified by the -H or --newhlq arguments.
        Equivalent to RENUNC command keyword.

    -o, --overwrite
        Overwrite existing datasets with the same name on the target device.
        Equivalent to REPUNC command keyword.

    -Q, --tmphlq=<TEMP_HLQ>
        Use an alternative high-level qualifier (HLQ) for the temporary dataset
        name. This overrides the TMPHLQ environment variable.

    --recatalog=<NEWCATNAME>

        Equivalent to RECATALOG command keyword. If this argument is not
        specified, then the CATALOG keyword is used instead.

        --recatalog=<NEWCATNAME>
            Catalogs the target dataset in the NEWCATNAME catalog.

        --recatalog=*
            Catalogs the target dataset in the same catalog that points to the
            source dataset. If the source dataset was not cataloged, the new
            dataset is not cataloged either.

    --rename=(<OLDNAME>,<NEWNAME>)
        Specifies that, if a dataset with the old name exists on the
        output DASD volume, DFSMSdss is to allocate a new dataset with the new
        name and restore the dataset. If the dataset with the old name does
        not exist on the volume, the dataset is restored with the old name.
        For a VSAM dataset that already exists on another DASD volume and is
        cataloged, the VSAM dataset is restored with the new name unless the
        new name exists and is cataloged.

        Use the --rename argument multiple times to specify multiple rename
        clauses. Equivalent to RENAME command keyword.

    -s, --size=<SIZE>
        Specifies how large to allocate datasets.
        Valid units are: CYL, TRK, K, M, G.
        Defaults to bytes if no unit is provided.

    -S, --storage-class=<STORAGE_CLASS>
        Specifies the user-desired storage class that is to replace the source
        storage class as input to the ACS routines. Equivalent to the
        STORCLAS command keyword.

    --share
        Specifies that DFSMSdss is to share, for read access with other
        programs, the datasets that are to be restored. The resetting of the
        dataset change indicator is bypassed if share is specified on a data
        set restore operation. Equivalent to the SHARE command keyword.

    --sphere
        Specifies that for any VSAM cluster dumped with the SPHERE keyword,
        DFSMSdss must also restore all associated AIX clusters and paths.
        Individual sphere component names need not be specified; only the base
        cluster name is required. Equivalent to the SPHERE keyword.

    -t, --to-volume=<TMPVOL>
        Specifies a particular volume will be used when temporary datasets are
        created.

    --target-gds=<STATUS>
        Restore non pre-allocated SMS-managed generation datasets with the
        specified status.

        STATUS may be one of the following: active, deferred, source, rolledoff.
        For a successful restore, the target generation data group (GDG) base
        must exist.

    --tolerate
        Specifies that target datasets are processed even though shared
        or exclusive access fails. Equivalent to the TOLERATE(ENQF) command
        keyword.

    -T, --dataset-type=<DATASET_TYPE>
        Specifies the type of dataset used for both the temporary and output
        datasets. This can be one of the following: LARGE, SEQ.
        Defaults to SEQ.

    -u, --use-sms-for-temps
        Specifies the SMS classes listed with the -S and -m arguments are used
        when creating temporary datasets.

    -v, --verbose
        Display verbose command information. Prints ADRDSSU restore command.

    -V, --volume
        Restore volume (default is dataset).

ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES

	TMPHLQ
        Overrides the current high-level qualifier (HLQ) used for temporary
        dataset names.

DESCRIPTION

    dunzip uses the ADRDSSU and AMATERSE utilities to unterse and restore
    datasets from an archive file or dataset created by dzip. Many of the
    arguments used with dunzip correspond to command keywords provided to
    ADRDSSU. For detailed explanations of the keywords, see this link:

    https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/zos/latest?topic=dfsmsdss-explanation-restore-command-keywords

USAGE NOTES

    By default dunzip modifies the HLQ of the dataset names restored to the
    user's HLQ. For example, the dataset OLDUSER.TEST.DS can be changed
    to NEWUSER.TEST.DS. To restore datasets with the original name, specify
    the -N or --no-rename argument. To restore datasets with a different HLQ,
    use the -H or --newhlq argument.

    All dataset and volume names provided to dzip are converted to uppercase.

    If you specify more than the first HLQ segment (such as MY.HLQ), the command
    exits with an error message.

    If you specify multiple dataset patterns with -e or -i, you must separate
    them with a space or comma (,).

DATASET PATTERNS

    The following rules apply to <DATASET_PATTERN>.

    Either fully or partially qualified dataset names can be used.

    Use a single asterisk (*) in place of exactly one qualifier.
    You can also use it to indicate to DFSMSdss that only part of a
    qualifier has been specified. For example, just the first, last,
    middle, or first and last parts.

    Using the double asterisk (**) with other qualifiers indicates either the
    nonexistence of leading, trailing, or middle qualifiers, or the fact that
    they play no role in the selection process.

    Two asterisks are the maximum permissible in a qualifier.
    If there are two asterisks in a qualifier, they must be the first and last
    characters.

    A question mark (?) or percent sign (%) matches a single character.

EXAMPLES

    Restore datasets from dzip file /tmp/zoau/dzp. All datasets are renamed
    to a new HLQ that matches the one of the current user ID.

        dunzip /tmp/zoau.dzp

    Restore datasets from the dzip file /tmp/zoau.dzp with their original names.

        dunzip --no-rename /tmp/zoau.dzp

    Restore datasets from the dzip file /tmp/zoau.dzp, but if dataset
    SOURCE.DATA.SET is present on the disk, rename it to SOURCE.NEW.DATA.SET.

        dunzip --rename=SOURCE.DATA.SET,SOURCE.NEW.DATA.SET /tmp/zoau.dzpy

    Restore datasets from the dzip file /tmp/zoau.dzp with a new HLQ of ZOAUSER.

        dunzip --newhlq=ZOAUSER /tmp/zoau.dzp

    Restore datasets from the dzip file /tmp/zoau.dzp onto volume TEST.
    The target dataset is created with a size of 5MB.

        dunzip -s 5M /tmp/zoau.dzp TEST

    Restore a volume from the dzip file /tmp/volume.dzp onto volume USER10.
    The volume is created with a size of 20GB.

        dunzip -V -s 20G /tmp/volume.dzp USER10

    Restore datasets from the dzip dataset SOURCE.DATA.SET onto volume USER10.
    Datasets with the pattern USER.TEST.* are included in the extraction,
    but the dataset USER.TEST.SOMEDS is excluded.

        dunzip -D -i user.test.* -e user.test.someds source.data.set USER10

    Restore datasets from the dzip file /tmp/zoau2.dzp, overwriting any
    existing datasets that have the same name. In addition, the FORCE command
    keyword is passed to ADRDSSU to allow an unmovable dataset or a dataset
    allocated by absolute track allocation to be moved.

        dunzip --overwrite --keyword="FORCE" /tmp/zoau2.dzp

EXIT VALUES

    0
        dunzip completed successfully.

    2
        The help text was printed.

    4
        There is a syntax error in the dunzip command.

    8
        An error occurred when dunzip was running.

